<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_tabletop_real_tee">
  <!-- Real lab T-block + goal, shared by every real-table scene. 89x99 mm
       plan, mass 0.1 kg, mu = 0.3 against the table. Keep in sync with
       SCENES["box_clutter_real"] etc. in oim/utils/scenes.py.

       Bodies sit at z = 0.0298, the geoms' own half-height, so a bottom
       face lands exactly on the table top at z = 0. They used to sit at a
       rounded 0.03 and hang 0.2 mm in the air -- an authoring slip, not a
       choice, though oim/runtime/mjcf.py had come to lean on it: with
       friction in the joints the block MUST NOT touch its support or the
       friction is counted twice, and that gap is what kept it from doing
       so. It called the gap "incidental geometry rather than a
       guarantee" at the time, and it was right. Now that friction IS the
       support contact the gap has to go: `tip_target_z` reads this body's
       z as the block's mid-height, so leaving it rounded put every tip
       height cost 0.2 mm off the block it is measured against.

       SUPPORT FRICTION IS THE TABLE CONTACT'S, not a `frictionloss` bound on
       the joints (which is what this file carried until now). The same switch
       ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop/tee.xml made for the simulated T on 2026-08-19,
       for the same reason and now that these scenes have a table to make it
       against.

       Why it matters here specifically: `frictionloss` is a FIXED force
       bound, so pressing down on the block costs nothing and the simulator
       cannot represent the one thing that makes top-riding expensive. The
       measurement on the simulated block, breakaway in +x against a downward
       press:

           press           0 N     5 N    10 N    20 N
           frictionloss   8.00    8.00    8.00    8.00   <- free
           contact mu     8.00   10.00   12.00   16.00   <- mu*(mg+F)

       The real driver's own top-riding problem is the same shape: the stick
       presses the block from above and slides it, and with `frictionloss`
       that strategy was unpriced in physics -- only ever discouraged by cost
       terms (`w_contact_z_exp`, `_tip_height_cost`), which is why raising
       those weights alone kept failing to move it.

       At zero press the two descriptions agree, which is what keeps
       mu*m*g = 0.2943 N meaningful as the NOMINAL budget that
       `PlanarPushingObject.wrench_limit` and every ADMM normalization read.
       The torque channel is NOT preserved -- a distributed patch resists
       rotation differently from the lumped 0.011772 N*m this file used to
       declare -- so `limit_surface_radius` in oim/utils/scenes.py is
       re-measured to match; see there. -->
  <worldbody>
    <body name="goal" mocap="true" pos="0.381 -0.305 0.0298" quat="0.70711 0 0 0.70711">
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_crossbar" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0"/>
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_stem" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0"/>
    </body>
    <body name="local_goal" mocap="true" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0298" quat="1 0 0 0">
      <geom class="local_goal" name="local_goal_crossbar" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0"/>
      <geom class="local_goal" name="local_goal_stem" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0"/>
    </body>
    <body name="block" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0298">
      <!-- No `frictionloss` any more: see the header. The planar resistance
           is the table contact's mu*N, declared in the <contact> block below. -->
      <joint name="T_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_z" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <!-- Vertical DoF, so the block has a normal force at all. Without it
           nothing presses the block into the table, mu*N is identically 0 and
           the block slides free. Declared AFTER T_x/T_y/T_z so the SE(2) pose
           stays at the first three qpos entries, which oim/tasks/pusht.py
           reads positionally (`block_qpos_adr`).

           z only, no roll/pitch, matching the simulated T: with tipping DoFs
           the block's contact blows up in the batched rollouts at
           planning_dt = 0.05. The block therefore stays level. -->
      <joint name="T_zs" type="slide" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <geom name="block_crossbar" contype="3" conaffinity="3" type="box" size="0.0445 0.0099 0.0298" pos="0.0 0.0099 0.0" mass="0.05" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
      <geom name="block_stem" contype="3" conaffinity="3" type="box" size="0.0099 0.0397 0.0298" pos="0.0 -0.0397 0.0" mass="0.05" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
  <!-- mu = 0.3, the lab's own measured object/table coefficient and the same
       mu the analytic limit surface assumes, so friction is mu*N and rises
       when the pusher presses down. `solimp` stiff for the same reason the
       simulated T's is: a soft normal constraint lets the block sink into the
       table under a press, which silently reduces N. Note mu here is the
       LAB's 0.3, not the simulated scene's 0.4 -- the mechanism is copied
       from there, the numbers are this table's. -->
  <contact>
    <pair geom1="block_crossbar" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
    <pair geom1="block_stem" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
  </contact>

  <sensor>
    <framepos name="position" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
    <framequat name="orientation" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>